McGuffin.Camp resides in Bell Buckle, Tennessee, with his wife Kathleen. They previously owned property in Valley Center, California.Camp is also known for his work with horses. He owns, and has trained, five horses who lived with him at his Valley Center Ranch. He has written a book about his experiences with horses, The Soul of a Horse.His autobiography, Underdog: How One Man Turned Hollywood Rejection into the Worldwide Phenomenon of Benji (published 1993) —republished as Benji and Me  in 2000—covers more than just his experience making \"Dog\" movies.\nJoe Camp is also the author of The Soul of a Horse: Life Lessons from the Herd, a 2008 book that tells his story of discovery after he and his wife bought their first horses.  \nHe owns a production company, Mulberry Square Productions, and had a minority interest in the film studio, Filmdallas Pictures.", "biography_author": "Wikidata", "date_of_biography": "2023-08-01T15:33:21.635778+10:00", "external_links": [{"id": 4350, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_Camp"}], "uuid": "0d07b93b-fff4-4207-bbfb-8858ef46a0ba", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "22028", "external_references": [{"id": 26200, "source": {"name": "Wikidata", "slug": "wikidata"}, "source_identifier": "Q1982336"}, {"id": 29433, "source": {"name": "VIAF", "slug": "viaf"}, "source_identifier": "39582837"}, {"id": 29434, "source": {"name": "LOC Auth", "slug": "loc-auth"}, "source_identifier": "n79019284"}, {"id": 29435, "source": {"name": "WorldCat", "slug": "worldcat"}, "source_identifier": "lccn-n79019284"}], "date_modified": "2023-09-05T21:29:41.982448+10:00"}]}